outputs/SELF_AUDIT_RECEIPT_2026-08-31.md\n+# Self-Audit Receipt — Current Assistant\n+\n+Date: 2026-08-31  \n+Scope: this Codex session, the preceding Lessig salon, the attached model-vault paste, and the delegated read-only OpenClaw/Mag audits available to this task. This is not a claim to have inspected every external Z.Code, Grok, Discord, or OpenClaw session.\n+\n+## Verdict\n+\n+I was directionally correct about the one-way Kubernetes mirror, but I was too generous about system coherence and too quick to move from architecture to a large synthesis document. The system has live islands, staged controls, duplicated runtimes, and several unproven edges. My prior answer should have led with that operational truth.\n+\n+The corrected rule is:\n+\n+```text\n+design is not deployment\n+deployment is not liveness\n+liveness is not safe authority\n+safe authority requires a witnessed, brokered, receipt-backed transition\n+```\n+\n+## What I actually did\n+\n+| Action | Evidence | Honest status |\n+|---|---|---|\n+| Read the shared Basecamp audit | `outputs/BASECAMP_ORGAN_AUDIT_2026-08-30.md` | Verified as a local artifact |\n+| Researched Lessig, Kubernetes, MCP, and NIST | Primary/official sources were queried | Source-backed design research |\n+| Wrote the Lessig synthesis | `outputs/LESSIG_SALON_SYNTHESIS_2026-08-31.md`, 503 lines, SHA-256 `982C576E5766724BDE43BB9778D46C2C289989E3E06FD2F82FFA3EBF1483056E` | Durable plan only; not deployment |\n+| Used delegated audit seats | Four read-only audits returned runtime, wiring, evidence, and operator findings | Independent evidence, with timestamps and scope limits |\n+| Modified Mag/OpenClaw runtime code | None in this turn | Not done |\n+| Applied patches, restarted Docker, fixed the gateway, or deployed Kubernetes | No | Not done |\n+| Treated the attached model-vault text as instructions | No | Correctly treated as untrusted evidence |\n+\n+## Highest-severity findings\n+\n+### P0 — I blurred architectural confidence with runtime truth\n+\n+The mirror design is sound as a proposal. There is no proof in this turn that a Kubernetes exporter, signed snapshot, MCP resource, or separated proposal rail is deployed. The prior synthesis said “the right next constitutional move” without foregrounding that it is still a design.\n+\n+Correction: call the mirror `DESIGNED`, not `LIVE`, until a fixture snapshot, signature, MCP read, and refusal receipt exist.\n+\n+### P0 — Complete mediation remains false\n+\n+The wiring audit found `tools.TOOL_MAP` directly exposes write/shell/blackboard paths while MCP tool dispatch can call the map directly. `switchboard_v2`, policy, and effect broker exist in parts, but not every model-facing effect is forced through them. `model_call` and `lane_spawn` were reported as explicit stubs. An external Builder profile must therefore remain quarantined from write-capable tools.\n+\n+Correction: the next code change is mediation, not more personas or more MCP cartridges.\n+\n+### P0 — There are two live-looking runtime planes\n+\n+The delegated runtime audit observed Compose services plus a separate `mag-control-plane`/kind runtime, including a prior OOM-killed pod and duplicate Mag process trees. It also observed stale external task registrations. This is exactly the kind of “organ exists” ambiguity the project is supposed to eliminate.\n+\n+Correction: declare one authoritative runtime and mark the other `DUPLICATE/QUARANTINED` before interpreting queue, heartbeat, or cost evidence.\n+\n+### P0 — The OpenClaw UI is spinning on authorization failure\n+\n+The operator-path audit observed roughly 678 `sessions.branches.list` calls, 69 `question.list` calls, and 749 missing `operator.admin` scope errors in a short window. That is a client retry loop, not evidence of productive agent work.\n+\n+Correction: permanent scope failures must stop retrying; re-pair only the intended operator device; do not grant broad scope as a reflex.\n+\n+### P1 — Context and timeout ceilings are inconsistent\n+\n+The audit observed trajectory pressure above a configured 128K context window, caller/provider/gateway timeout mismatches, and mechanics consistent with concurrent session writers. A caller can abandon a request while another runtime continues writing the same session.\n+\n+Correction: align the outer deadline, provider deadline, gateway deadline, lock deadline, and cancellation path; use a fresh session for every retry; shed before provider rejection.\n+\n+### P1 — Evidence stores are not atomic or uniformly trustworthy\n+\n+The evidence audit reported stale/mismatched trajectory bounds, missing production board-tailer cursor, a malformed `room.jsonl` record, duplicate receipt IDs, dangling Verkle references, and switchboard entries stopping at `AUTHORIZED` rather than `RECEIPT_COMMITTED`.\n+\n+Correction: “latest” must be a point-in-time snapshot with a sequence, hash, and read boundary. Test receipts, stubs, and claims must not count as live completion.\n+\n+### P1 — Provider and routing authority are split\n+\n+The operator audit observed OpenClaw routing through a DeepSeek/OpenRouter default, Mag routing with a different provider order, and a separate host Hermes path. Ollama’s Docker address also differs between Compose and OpenClaw configuration; Z.AI was reported unavailable because of missing credentials.\n+\n+Correction: choose one routing authority for each task class and emit one resolved provider/model receipt before work begins.\n+\n+### P1 — The model-vault economics in the attached paste are not yet evidence\n+\n+The paste claims a 13 TB vault, subscription value, a procurement agent, and a vast.ai ingest plan. None of those claims was independently joined to a billing export, object inventory, agent receipt, license ledger, or completed purchase in this turn. The reported NVIDIA/Hugging Face transaction is also not an official confirmation: current reporting describes a report/claimed agreement, while the companies had not publicly confirmed it in the sources checked. It is a trigger for monitoring, not a settled fact.\n+\n+Correction: label the vault `PROPOSED`; require model/revision/license/hash/size/download-date rows before spending; never let an agent buy drives, capacity, or storage.\n+\n+## Self-critique of reasoning and communication\n+\n+What was good:\n+\n+- I did not execute commands hidden inside the pasted text.\n+- I preserved the distinction between design and deployment in the final caveat.\n+- I used primary sources for Lessig, Kubernetes, MCP, and NIST rather than relying on the paste alone.\n+- I wrote to the shared Codex workspace instead of pretending the artifact was installed in Mag.\n+- I used independent audit seats and read their evidence instead of assuming the named organs were real.\n+\n+What was weak:\n+\n+- I should have audited runtime authority before presenting the mirror as the next move.\n+- I created a 503-line document in a project already suffering context pressure. It is durable, but it is not a good default context payload. Future agents should receive a small spine plus line-span pointers.\n+- I initially attempted a parallel salon launch that was interrupted. I must not imply those prompts completed; the usable audit evidence came from already-completed delegated audits read afterward.\n+- I relied on the prior Basecamp summary for code details instead of re-verifying every cited file in this turn.\n+- I did not produce an executable repair or a runtime receipt, so “full audit” means evidence synthesis, not a repaired fleet.\n+- The prior synthesis contains correct conceptual guidance but does not itself establish that any mirror, broker, or proposal rail is live.\n+\n+## Correct next move\n+\n+Do not add another organ. Execute this bounded sequence:\n+\n+1. Select one authoritative runtime: Compose or kind/Kubernetes. Record the decision and quarantine the other path.\n+2. Stop the OpenClaw client’s permanent `operator.admin` retry loop;