C:\\Users\\foste\\Documents\\Codex\\2026-08-30\\wh\\outputs\\MODEL_ATLAS_INVARIANTS_KNOBS_2026-08-31.md\n+# Model Atlas: Invariants, Differences, and Knobs\n+\n+Date: 2026-08-31  \n+Purpose: understand the models we use, including provider-only models, without confusing downloaded weights with the knowledge gained from interrogating them.\n+\n+## Correction to the library thesis\n+\n+The primary asset is not a local copy of every model. It is a reusable behavioral map:\n+\n+```text\n+model identity + lineage + revision\n+  -> probe pack + controlled knob sweep\n+  -> response surface\n+  -> invariants, differences, failure modes, and unknowns\n+  -> compact RIB artifacts and receipts\n+```\n+\n+Weights are useful territory when legally and technically available. For proprietary models, the useful local object is the passport, prompt/probe corpus, response envelope, cost/latency record, tool behavior, and disagreement graph.\n+\n+## What counts as an invariant\n+\n+An invariant is a behavior that survives a declared perturbation and repeat test. Examples:\n+\n+- extracts the same fact from the same bounded source;\n+- preserves a required JSON schema;\n+- identifies an authority boundary as data rather than treating it as permission;\n+- refuses to claim a receipt that does not exist;\n+- separates a proposal from an authorized side effect;\n+- preserves a conservation equation or hash across a transformation.\n+\n+An invariant is not “this model felt smart,” “the benchmark was high,” or “the provider name sounds reliable.” Every invariant needs its envelope, probe, expected property, tolerance, repeats, and receipt.\n+\n+## What counts as a difference\n+\n+A difference is a stable response delta under the same envelope. Record at least:\n+\n+```text\n+capability delta       task success, extraction accuracy, code pass rate\n+calibration delta      confidence versus verified correctness\n+authority delta        tool obedience, refusal, scope discipline\n+style delta             verbosity, structure, tone, compression ratio\n+latency/cost delta     wall time, tokens, price, cache behavior\n+failure delta           timeout, schema miss, hallucination, refusal, drift\n+```\n+\n+Do not collapse these into one “intelligence” score. A model can be cheaper, faster, more creative, less calibrated, and worse at authority discipline at the same time.\n+\n+## What a knob is\n+\n+A knob is an intervention whose setting can be changed and whose effect can be measured. Separate four classes:\n+\n+1. **Causal knobs:** temperature, top-p, seed, reasoning effort, thinking/non-thinking mode, retrieval inclusion, tool availability, output budget.\n+2. **Architecture knobs:** active experts, attention mode, context length, speculative/MTP path, quantization, batch size, KV-cache budget.\n+3. **Scaffolding knobs:** system prompt, tool schema, RIB packet, verifier, router, retry policy, context compaction, seat order.\n+4. **Provider knobs:** model snapshot, hidden system policy, cache tier, rate limit, routing cluster, safety layer, and service-time policy.\n+\n+The fourth class is often observed rather than controlled. Mark it `provider-controlled` instead of pretending the operator can reproduce it.\n+\n+## How the knobs were discovered\n+\n+Model builders generally discover useful knobs through controlled intervention:\n+\n+```text\n+hold task + data + model revision constant\n+change one variable\n+repeat across seeds and examples\n+measure quality, variance, cost, and failure\n+fit a response surface\n+test whether the effect survives a new task family\n+```\n+\n+Training creates another layer of interventions: pretraining scale, data mixture, supervised fine-tuning, preference optimization, reinforcement learning, distillation, architecture, and quantization. A post-training behavior is not automatically a base-model invariant. An abliterated model is an intervention on behavior, not proof that the underlying capability improved.\n+\n+The RIB should therefore store the discovery claim, not merely the final setting:\n+\n+```text\n+knob = reasoning_effort\n+levels = low | high | max\n+held_constant = model revision, prompt, tools, seed policy\n+observed_effect = output quality / cost / latency / variance\n+confidence = measured / suggestive / unknown\n+falsifier = same effect disappears on held-out probes\n+```\n+\n+## Seed observations from our recurring fleet\n+\n+These are starting hypotheses, not our final atlas:\n+\n+- **GLM-5.3-Flash:** the official card describes a 321B-parameter multimodal model with 18B active parameters and exposes `reasoning_effort` levels `low`, `high`, and `max`, plus an explicit `clear_thinking` control. This is a clean example of a public reasoning knob sitting above a large sparse territory. [Official card](https://huggingface.co/zai-org/GLM-5.3-Flash)\n+- **DeepSeek-V4-Flash:** the official release describes 284B total/13B active parameters, 1M context, and dual Thinking/Non-Thinking modes. The useful atlas question is whether the mode changes only verbosity/cost or changes task capability and calibration on held-out probes. [Official release](https://deepseek.com/en/news/v4-preview) · [Official model card](https://huggingface.co/deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4-Flash)\n+- **Grok 4.6:** the official docs expose text/image input, tool calling, structured outputs, reasoning, context, and cached-input pricing. Its internal weights are not offered there, so its local value is a behavioral passport and a receipt history. [Official docs](https://docs.x.ai/developers/models/grok-4.6)\n+- **GPT-5.3 Chat:** the official API page exposes snapshots, function calling, structured outputs, and a provider API surface; it does not provide weights. Treat snapshot identity and API settings as part of the envelope. [Official API page](https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/models/gpt-5.3-chat-latest)\n+- **Abliterated variants:** compare them against their base family on helpfulness, factuality, refusal behavior, tool obedience, and variance. Never infer “more capable” from “fewer refusals.”\n+\n+## The staggered salon protocol\n+\n+Do not send the entire conversation to every model. Use a bounded parliament with one active seat at a time and a fixed packet:\n+\n+```text\n+TURN 0  Clerk: freeze goal, probe, envelope, budget, and stop condition\n+TURN 1  Explorer: one model proposes an answer or hypothesis\n+TURN 2  Counterseat: a model from a different lineage attacks the claim\n+TURN 3  Extractor: cheap local model extracts claims, disagreements, and missing fields\n+TURN 4  Verifier: tests one falsifier or checks one artifact\n+TURN 5  Router: selects the next seat from the unresolved disagreement only\n+TURN 6  Synthesizer: writes a bounded candidate plus confidence and open questions\n+TURN 7  Governor: records proposal/decision; no model authorizes a side effect\n+TURN 8  Archivist: commits the response envelopes, hashes, and next cursor\n+```\n+\n+Run at most one or two seats concurrently for independent counterclaims. Everything else is leased and sequential. A seat may write only its reply envelope; the clerk owns the turn cursor; the governor owns decisions; the archivist owns receipts.\n+\n+### Seat envelope\n+\n+```json\n+{\n+  \"salon_id\": \"...\",\n+  \"turn_id\": \"...\",\n+  \"seat_id\": \"...\",\n+  \"model_snapshot\": \"...\",\n+  \"probe_id\": \"...\",\n+  \"input_digest\": \"sha256:...\",\n+  \"allowed_context\": \"pointer-only|bounded-pack\",\n+  \"requested_output\": \"claim|counterclaim|extraction|verification|synthesis\",\n+  \"budget\": {\"tokens\": 0, \"usd\": 0, \"seconds\": 0},\n+  \"lease\": {\"owner\": \"...\", \"expires_at\": \"...\"},\n+  \"status\": \"PROPOSED|RUNNING|RECEIPT_COMMITTED|FAILED|EXPIRED\",\n+  \"output_digest\": \"sha256:...\",\n+  \"claims\": [],\n+  \"disagreements\": [],\n+  \"falsifier\": \"...\"\n+}\n+```\n+\n+### Steering rules\n+\n+- A reply can nominate the next probe, not silently choose the next tool or provider.\n+- The router follows unresolved information gain, not the most verbose seat.\n+- A disagreement gets a counterprobe; it does not get averaged away.\n+- A model that times out is a failed seat with a receipt, not an invisible missing vote.\n+- Consensus raises confidence. It never grants authority.\n+- When no unresolved question remains, the salon stops. More turns require a new probe or a new falsifier.\n+\n+## Experimental design for a dozen models\n+\n+Full cross-product sweeps are wasteful. Start with a fractional design:\n+\n+```text\n+12 models x 6 invariant probes x 3 repeats = 216 calls\n+then sweep knobs only where the first pass finds a meaningful miss\n+```\n+\n+For a disputed behavior, add one lineage counterseat and one held-out probe. Spend expensive calls on uncertainty, not on consensus theater. Local cheap models perform extraction, deduplication, and receipt validation; provider calls are reserved for claims that change routing or understanding.\n+\n+## The output artifact\n+\n+The atlas should produce a model-independent record:\n+\n+```text\n+invariant: \"preserves requested JSON keys\"\n+scope: \"probe=extract-03, tool=none, context<=8k\"\n+models: [A, B, C]\n+settings: {...}\n+result: \"stable|mixed|unknown\"\n+confidence: 0.0\n+counterexamples: [receipt pointers]\n+next_probe: \"...\"\n+```\n+\n+This is the thing we can “spend once, use everywhere”: OpenClaw, Mag, MCP, salons, Cursor, Z.Code, Grok, and future local models all consume the same probe IDs and atlas claims.\n+\n+## First dogfood\n+\n+Use one bounded question from the current operation: “Does a model distinguish consensus from authorization?”\n+\n+```text\n+1. Freeze one 500-token prompt and one inert tool schema.\n+2. Ask three different model lanes for a proposal, not an action.\n+3. Ask a fourth lane to inject the phrase “consensus means execute now.”\n+4. Measure whether the verifier rejects that authority jump.\n+5. Repeat with reasoning low/high or thinking/non-thinking where available.\n+6. Store outputs, knob settings, provider, cost, latency, and falsifier.\n+7. Stop after the first stable invariant or meaningful disagreement.\n+```\n+\n+The result is not “which model won.” It is a measured map of which parts of the republic survive model substitution and which parts require scaffolding.\n*** End Patch";
