C:\\Users\\foste\\Documents\\Codex\\2026-08-30\\wh\\outputs\\MODEL_ATLAS_INVARIANTS_KNOBS_2026-08-31.md\n@@\n The result is not “which model won.” It is a measured map of which parts of the republic survive model substitution and which parts require scaffolding.\n+\n+## Game-world lens\n+\n+Treat every model snapshot plus its runtime envelope as a world instance, not merely a contestant:\n+\n+```text\n+world seed       model snapshot, tokenizer, system policy, tools, runtime\n+physics           stable capabilities, constraints, refusals, schema behavior\n+terrain           domains where the model is strong, weak, or unmeasured\n+weather           latency, quota, context pressure, provider drift\n+portals           API, MCP, tool contract, retrieval, file surfaces\n+levers            sampling, reasoning mode, context, tool availability\n+quests            fixed probes with a falsifier and a bounded reward\n+map               atlas passport, claims, hashes, receipts, uncertainty\n+fog               hidden system prompts, undocumented routing, unknown lineage\n+death             timeout, context overflow, schema failure, false completion\n+```\n+\n+The objective is not to make every world look like the home world. It is to discover which laws travel:\n+\n+```text\n+enter world -> run a known quest -> perturb one lever\n+             -> observe terrain/weather -> record a receipt\n+             -> extract a portable mechanic -> test it in another world\n+```\n+\n+This turns “model mining” into experimental cartography. We mine mechanics, not personalities: how a model compresses, reasons, refuses, calls tools, handles contradiction, preserves a schema, and reacts to context pressure. We do not call a behavior universal until it survives a lineage change, a knob change, and a held-out quest.\n+\n+### World passport\n+\n+```json\n+{\n+  \"world_id\": \"provider/model@snapshot\",\n+  \"seed\": {\"model\": \"...\", \"revision\": \"...\", \"runtime\": \"...\"},\n+  \"laws_observed\": [],\n+  \"terrain_profile\": {\"code\": null, \"research\": null, \"vision\": null},\n+  \"weather\": {\"latency_ms\": null, \"quota\": null, \"context_pressure\": null},\n+  \"levers_tested\": [],\n+  \"portals\": [\"api\", \"mcp\", \"tools\"],\n+  \"quest_receipts\": [],\n+  \"unknowns\": [],\n+  \"status\": \"charted|partial|unmapped|quarantined\"\n+}\n+```\n+\n+### Expedition party\n+\n+The staggered salon is a small expedition party, not a crowd shouting in a tavern:\n+\n+```text\n+cartographer  freezes the base map and cursor\n+scout         enters one world and reports observations\n+counterscout  tests a competing explanation\n+miner         extracts claims and reusable mechanics\n+warden        tests authority, tool scope, and false completion\n+quartermaster records cost, latency, context, and resource use\n+governor      chooses the next quest; never delegates its authority\n+archivist     commits the receipt and updates the map\n+```\n+\n+Each seat gets one bounded turn and a small packet. The next seat receives pointers and the unresolved question, not the entire conversation. A world can be strange; the expedition cannot be unbounded.\n+\n+### What becomes portable\n+\n+- probe definitions and falsifiers;\n+- normalized claim schemas;\n+- tool and authority contracts;\n+- context-admission rules;\n+- cost/latency/weather measurements;\n+- successful prompt or RIB patterns, with provenance;\n+- counterexamples and failure receipts;\n+- adapters that translate a world’s local dialect into the republic’s common envelope.\n+\n+What does not become portable without evidence is a vibe, a benchmark ranking, a provider’s hidden system behavior, or a model’s apparent persona.\n+\n+The Dirac-like move is to preserve the transition, not merely the state:\n+\n+```text\n+world entered -> quest issued -> lever changed -> observation made\n+              -> claim extracted -> counterexample checked -> map updated\n+```\n+\n+If the transition cannot be replayed from its envelope and receipt, it is a rumor from the tavern, not a law of the republic.\n*** End Patch";
